    <link:footnote xlink:type="resource" xlink:label="fn-15" id="fn-15" xml:lang="en" xlink:role="http://www.xbrl.org/2003/role/footnote">
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" style="text-align:justify">
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%">We have a net excess deferred income tax regulatory liability resulting from the implementation of the Federal Tax Cut and Jobs Act of 2017.
          <br/>
          <br/>We use RSGM for the amortization of (excess)/deficient deferred income taxes to customers. The deferred taxes associated with public utility plant property will be amortized over a period ranging from 45 to 60 years and deferred taxes not related to public utility property will be amortized over a period of 48 years. The net amortization of (excess)/deficient deferred income taxes is $(358,799).
          <br/>
          <br/>The total net (excess)/deficient deferred income tax amortization of $(376,543) is reported as the input to page 3, line 73a in our formula rate tariff.
        </span>
      </div>
    </link:footnote>
    <link:footnote xlink:type="resource" xlink:label="fn-16" id="fn-16" xml:lang="en" xlink:role="http://www.xbrl.org/2003/role/footnote">
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" style="text-align:justify">
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%">We have a net excess deferred income tax regulatory liability due to enacted legislation in the states of Oklahoma and Kansas to reduce the corporate tax rates. 
          <br/>
          <br/>We use RSGM for the amortization of (excess)/deficient deferred income taxes to customers. The deferred taxes associated with Oklahoma will be amortized over a period of 48 years. The net amortization of (excess)/deficient deferred income taxes is $(4,185). The deferred taxes associated with the Kansas tax rate change will be amortized over a period of 46 years. The net amortization of (excess)/deficient deferred income taxes is $(21,000). Additionally, we have a net deficient deferred income tax regulatory asset as a result of the annual change in state apportionment factors. We amortize (excess)/deficient deferred income taxes related to apportionment shifts over a one-year period. The amortization of the net (excess)/deficient deferred income taxes is $7,441.
          <br/>
        </span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" style="text-align:justify">
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%">
          <br/>The total net (excess)/deficient deferred income tax amortization of $(376,543) is reported as the input to page 3, line 73a in our formula rate tariff.
        </span>
      </div>
    </link:footnote>
    <link:footnote xlink:type="resource" xlink:label="fn-17" id="fn-17" xml:lang="en" xlink:role="http://www.xbrl.org/2003/role/footnote">
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" style="text-align:justify">
        <span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%">Pursuant to a Settlement Agreement filed and approve by FERC under Docket No. ER09-548-002 and EC11-108-001, the Start-up and Development Regulatory Assets, the KETA Project Regulatory Asset and the Kansas V-Plan Project Regulatory Asset are amortized monthly straight-line over 10-year beginning April 2015. Amortization expense for the principal, debt carrying charges, equity carrying charges and income taxes on equity carrying charges for each regulatory asset are recorded within Account No. 566, Miscellaneous Transmission Expenses. The portion of amortization expense recorded to Account No. 566 associated with the regulatory assets are shown below:</span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" style="text-align:justify">
        <span>
          <br/>
        </span>
      </div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" style="text-align:justify">
